---
/*
  How far through the post you are, drawn along the bottom of the window.

  WHAT IT MEASURES, AND WHY THAT IS NOT THE PAGE

  The obvious implementation tracks the document: scrollY over
  scrollHeight - innerHeight. On this site that would be wrong at both ends. A
  post is followed by a related-work grid, an email capture, a "more posts"
  row and a footer that now carries the site's whole index - several screens
  of things that are not the post. A document-scoped bar would sit at maybe
  40% when the last sentence has been read, and only fill once the reader has
  scrolled through everything after it.

  So it measures the <article>: 0 when its top meets the top of the window,
  100% when its bottom meets the bottom. Reaching the end of the bar and
  reaching the end of the writing are the same moment, which is the only thing
  a reading indicator is for.

  It hides when the article is not on screen at all - before it and after it
  there is nothing to report, and a bar stuck at 100% over the footer is a
  progress bar for a thing you have finished.

  A SHORT POST HAS NO PROGRESS. When the article fits inside the window there
  is no scrolling to track, and the arithmetic below would divide by zero. It
  stays hidden instead of showing a bar that is either always empty or always
  full.

  STYLE

  Square, hairline track, solid fill, no radius - the same construction as the
  archive switch and the plate strip's scroll line, which are the other two
  places this site draws a bar with a fill on it. Nothing new invented for it.

  aria-hidden: it reports the scroll position of a page the reader is already
  scrolling. Announcing "37%" as it changes would be noise, and there is no
  control here to operate.
*/
---

<div class="read-bar" aria-hidden="true">
  <span class="read-bar__fill"></span>
</div>

<script>
  const bar = document.querySelector<HTMLElement>('.read-bar');
  const article = document.querySelector<HTMLElement>('.blog-article');

  if (bar && article) {
    let queued = false;

    const measure = () => {
      queued = false;
      const box = article.getBoundingClientRect();

      /*
        The scroll distance over which the article is being read: from its top
        meeting the top of the window to its bottom meeting the bottom. Zero or
        less means the whole article already fits on screen.
      */
      const travel = article.offsetHeight - window.innerHeight;
      const onScreen = box.top < window.innerHeight && box.bottom > 0;

      if (travel <= 0 || !onScreen) {
        bar.classList.remove('is-reading');
        return;
      }

      // box.top is negative once the article's top has passed the top of the
      // window, which is exactly how far into it the reader has scrolled.
      const read = Math.min(Math.max(-box.top / travel, 0), 1);
      bar.classList.add('is-reading');
      bar.style.setProperty('--read', String(read));
    };

    /*
      rAF-throttled rather than debounced, the same as the plate strip's scroll
      line: a progress bar that arrives after the scroll has stopped is worse
      than none, because it contradicts the thing it is drawing. The flag
      refuses to queue a second read while one is pending, so it is naturally
      capped at one measurement per frame.
    */
    const schedule = () => {
      if (queued) return;
      queued = true;
      requestAnimationFrame(measure);
    };

    addEventListener('scroll', schedule, { passive: true });
    // Rotating a phone or the URL bar collapsing changes innerHeight, which is
    // half of the arithmetic above.
    addEventListener('resize', schedule, { passive: true });
    // A late image can still change the article's height under it.
    addEventListener('load', schedule);
    // DOMContentLoaded does not fire on a back/forward restore; pageshow does,
    // and the scroll position comes back with it.
    addEventListener('pageshow', schedule);
    measure();
  }
</script>
